## Project context

This project spans two TypeScript codelines, each with vitest as its test runner:

- **mocka** — `/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a`, fare logic in `src/fares.ts`, tests in `test/fares.test.ts`
- **mockb** — `/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b`, schedule rendering in `src/schedule.ts` (including `formatStops`), tests in `test/schedule.test.ts`

Two tickets are in scope, both single-line pure-function logic fixes:

- **MOCK3-1** — riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult fare (a boundary comparison in `src/fares.ts`)
- **MOCK3-2** — the departure board omits the final stop (a loop bound in `formatStops`, `src/schedule.ts`)

Diff paths will be relative to each codeline root, so the same relative path (`src/…`, `test/…`) can appear under either mocka or mockb. Attribute each finding to the correct codeline and report the file path exactly as the injected diff spells it.

## The trap this stack presents

Both fixes are off-by-one boundary edits. The failure mode this gate exists to catch is a changed comparison operator or loop bound with **no corresponding boundary case added to the codeline's test file** — a diff that touches `src/fares.ts` but not `test/fares.test.ts`, or `src/schedule.ts` but not `test/schedule.test.ts`. Treat that pairing as the primary signal under category 5 (test coverage gaps), and read any operator or bound change in the diff (`<` vs `<=`, `>` vs `>=`, `length` vs `length - 1`) as a deliberate boundary edit whose correctness rests on which side of the boundary the ticket says belongs where: age exactly 65 is not the adult fare, and the final stop belongs on the board.

Because these are pure functions, a boundary change is fully determinable from the diff hunk itself — do not report it as unverifiable if the hunk shows the operator or bound in context.

Note also that these are one-line fixes in small pure functions. Do not manufacture complexity or duplication findings from a two-line diff; category 1 and category 2 will legitimately be empty on a change of this size. Naming under category 6 follows ordinary TypeScript convention in these codelines: camelCase variables and functions (as in `formatStops`), PascalCase types, UPPER_SNAKE constants.

